// A `foreach` binding may shadow an enclosing local of its name (the shadowing ban is on DECLARATIONS —
// see tests/xfail/constparam_shadow_foreach.kama for the one binder exemption that is not allowed), and
// inside the loop the name is the element, whole.
//
// The binding writes no `_localCTypes` entry, by design, so the enclosing `string m`'s entry kept
// answering for it and `takeE(v: m)` was refused for handing a `string`. Now the binder HIDES the
// enclosing entry for the loop's extent; the scope's snapshot brings it back at the closing brace, so
// `m.length()` after the loop still sees the string.
type value E { public int32 n; public ctor of(int32 n) { this.n = n; } }
fn int32 takeE(E v) { return v.n; }
fn int32 main() {
    InlineArray<E>#(2) xs = [E.of(n: 2), E.of(n: 3)];
    int32 r = 0;
    string m = "boom";
    foreach (E m in xs) { r = r + takeE(v: m); }   // 5 — the element
    return r + cast<int32>(m.length());            // + 4 — the string again
}
